// 01 · Post-Practice Cognitive Deficit: the post-practice "fog" is brief; what lingers is
// thirst and short sleep. Composition: hook = small figure bottom-right in an empty field,
// one-line headline top-left; takeaway = line top-left, question flush right below;
// alignment = left.

module.exports = {
  num: 1,
  slug: "post-practice-cognitive-deficit",
  title: "Post-Practice Cognitive Deficit",
  category: "Contextual Engineering & Education Delivery",
  palette: "night",
  hero: { layout: "dip-recovery-curve", concept: "One line of thinking sharpness that dips briefly at the end of hard practice and comes back within minutes; a second, dashed line stays low when the athlete is dehydrated or short on sleep" },
  compose: { hook: "small-figure-field", takeaway: "question-right", align: "left" },
  oneIdea: "Hard practice doesn't fog the brain for long. Dehydration and short sleep do, so refuel first, then teach.",
  question: "What do we try to teach in the first five minutes after practice?",
  caveats: `The topic is often pitched as "after hard exercise, working memory and processing speed drop significantly." The research doesn't support that as a general rule. Across 79 studies, a single bout of exercise gave thinking a small boost on average, including right after (Chang et al. 2012). After very hard exercise the results are mixed; in Sudo et al.'s review, impairments were only reported when testing happened within about six minutes. Present the six minutes as what that review observed, not as a measured threshold. Those studies used short lab bouts (mostly 5-60 minutes of cycling or running in comfortable rooms), not a two-and-a-half-hour practice in Texas heat; long, hot sessions are less studied, and dehydration is the likeliest reason they would differ. The dehydration and sleep findings come from lab studies in mostly non-athlete adults. The curve on slide 3 is a drawn shape, labeled as illustrative. Don't promise a number of minutes to wait; the practical rule is water, food and cooling down first.`,
  sources: [
    { id: "chang2012", cite: "Chang YK, Labban JD, Gapin JI, Etnier JL. The effects of acute exercise on cognitive performance: a meta-analysis. Brain Res. 2012;1453:87-101. doi:10.1016/j.brainres.2012.02.068", grade: "meta-analysis (79 studies)", found: "external", supports: "Overall effect of a single exercise bout on cognition was small and positive (g = 0.097), including immediately after exercise (g = 0.108)." },
    { id: "sudo2022", cite: "Sudo M, Costello JT, McMorris T, Ando S. The effects of acute high-intensity aerobic exercise on cognitive performance: a structured narrative review. Front Behav Neurosci. 2022;16:957677. doi:10.3389/fnbeh.2022.957677", grade: "structured narrative review of lab studies", found: "external", supports: "Results after high-intensity exercise are mixed; 'no impairments were reported when the timing of cognitive tasks was > 6 min after exercise.'" },
    { id: "wittbrodt2018", cite: "Wittbrodt MT, Millard-Stafford M. Dehydration impairs cognitive performance: a meta-analysis. Med Sci Sports Exerc. 2018;50(11):2360-2368. doi:10.1249/MSS.0000000000001682", grade: "meta-analysis (33 studies)", found: "external", supports: "Dehydration impairs attention, executive function and motor coordination; impairment was greater in studies reporting more than 2% body mass loss." },
    { id: "fullagar2015", cite: "Fullagar HHK, Skorski S, Duffield R, Hammes D, Coutts AJ, Meyer T. Sleep and athletic performance: the effects of sleep loss on exercise performance, and physiological and cognitive responses to exercise. Sports Med. 2015;45(2):161-186. doi:10.1007/s40279-014-0260-0", grade: "narrative review", found: "Zotero", supports: "Section 4.3: when sleep is cut to under 7 hours, healthy adults do worse on tests of alertness, reaction time and memory." },
    { id: "skotniczny2026", cite: "Skotniczny K, Toborek M, Terbalyan A, Zajac A, Gawełczyk M, Siatkowski S, Chycki J. Pre-exercise hydration modulates the sweat rate and executive control during moderate exercise to 3% dehydration under thermoneutral conditions. J Hum Kinet. 2026;101:285-303. doi:10.5114/jhk/220097", grade: "small randomized lab study (n = 30)", found: "Zotero", supports: "Men who started a long ride under-hydrated were slower on a focus task (Stroop interference) afterward; working memory was unchanged." },
  ],
  slides: [
    {
      role: "hook", time: 45,
      notes: `A lot of us work on the assumption that athletes come off the field cooked, and that anything we teach in that window is wasted. Some of us have stopped teaching after practice for exactly that reason.

The research on hard exercise and thinking is bigger than you might expect. Seventy-nine studies, pooled together, found that a single bout of exercise gives thinking a small lift on average, and that holds right after exercise too. So the fog, if it shows up at all, is shorter than we think. Then why do post-practice meetings so often fall flat?`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("The fog after practice lifts fast.", { y: 1.2, w: 11.5, h: 0.9, size: 48 });
        s.lead("Studies of single exercise bouts. On average, thinking got slightly better, even right after.", { x: 4.9, y: 4.55, w: 4.3, h: 1.3, size: 22, align: "right" });
        s.figure("79", { x: 9.4, y: 4.0, w: 3.18, h: 1.9, size: 150, align: "right", refs: ["chang2012"] });
        s.source("Chang et al., Brain Research, 2012", { x: 7.6, y: 5.95, w: 4.98, align: "right", refs: ["chang2012"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "problem", time: 60,
      notes: `Here's what does hold up. The things that come with practice are what slow athletes down afterward.

Dehydration is the big one. A meta-analysis of thirty-three studies found it hurts attention, decision tasks and coordination, and the drop was bigger once people had lost more than two percent of their body weight in sweat. In a small study from Eric's own library, men who started a long ride already under-hydrated were slower on a focus task at the end of it.

Sleep is the other. Under seven hours, alertness, reaction time and memory all suffer. Put a short night together with a hot practice and that's the athlete sitting in your meeting.`,
      build(s) {
        s.rect(8.95, 0, 4.383, 7.5, { fill: "soft" });
        s.headline("What lingers after practice: thirst and short sleep.", { y: 1.2, w: 7.5, h: 1.65, size: 46 });
        const rows = [
          ["Dehydration dulls attention and decisions.", "Wittbrodt & Millard-Stafford 2018"],
          ["Under 7 hours of sleep slows reaction time and memory.", "Fullagar et al. 2015"],
        ];
        rows.forEach(([t, src], i) => {
          const y = 3.35 + i * 1.45;
          s.line(0.75, y, 8.1, y, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
          s.text(0.75, y + 0.2, 7.35, 0.8, t, { font: "light", size: 26, lineH: 1.1 });
          s.source(src, { x: 0.75, y: y + 0.98, w: 7.35, h: 0.32, size: 13, valign: "top" });
        });
        s.figure("2%", { x: 9.55, y: 2.55, w: 3.0, h: 1.5, size: 130, color: "shape", refs: ["wittbrodt2018"] });
        s.text(9.6, 4.25, 2.95, 1.6, "of body weight lost in sweat. Past it, the drop gets bigger.", { font: "light", size: 22, lineH: 1.2 });
        s.cite("fullagar2015", "skotniczny2026");
      },
    },
    {
      role: "insight", time: 70,
      notes: `So here's the picture. The solid line is thinking sharpness. At the end of a really hard session there can be a dip. But in the lab studies, the dip is short. One review found no impairment reported once testing started more than about six minutes after hard exercise. Most of the time the line is back where it started before anyone has finished their water.

The dashed line is the athlete who is dehydrated or ran on five hours of sleep. Their dip doesn't come back on its own, because the cause is still there.

One honest limit: those studies used short bouts in cool labs, not a long practice in the heat. That's another reason to put water first.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("The dip is brief, unless something keeps it down.", { y: 1.2, w: 11.5, h: 0.8, size: 40 });
        const ox = 0.95, w = 11.4, base = 3.25, low = 5.55;
        const X = f => ox + f * w;
        const t0 = 0.24;
        s.rect(X(t0), 2.45, X(0.36) - X(t0), 3.45, { fill: "soft" });
        s.line(X(0), base, X(1), base, { color: "rule", w: 1, dash: true });
        s.text(X(0), base - 0.5, 3.2, 0.4, "Usual sharpness", { font: "light", size: 16, color: "muted" });
        s.line(X(t0), 2.45, X(t0), 5.9, { color: "muted", w: 1 });
        s.text(X(t0) - 3.05, 5.5, 2.95, 0.4, "Practice ends", { font: "cond", size: 18, color: "muted", align: "right" });
        const dip = [[0, 0], [0.18, 0], [0.24, 0.1], [0.27, 0.62], [0.3, 0.52], [0.34, 0.12], [0.4, 0.01], [0.55, 0], [1, 0]];
        const linger = [[0.24, 0.1], [0.27, 0.68], [0.32, 0.72], [0.42, 0.66], [0.6, 0.64], [0.8, 0.63], [1, 0.62]];
        const P = arr => arr.map(([f, d]) => [X(f), base + d * (low - base)]);
        s.path(P(linger), { smooth: true, color: "muted", w: 2.5, dash: true });
        s.path(P(dip), { smooth: true, color: "accent", w: 5 });
        s.text(X(0.25), 2.5, 2.3, 0.4, "First few minutes", { font: "cond", size: 18, color: "muted" });
        s.text(X(0.5), base + 0.2, 4.6, 0.45, "Rested and hydrated: back to usual", { font: "condBold", size: 20, color: "accent" });
        s.text(X(1) - 4.6, low + 0.12, 4.6, 0.45, "Dehydrated or short on sleep: stays low", { font: "cond", size: 20, color: "ink", align: "right" });
        s.text(X(0), 5.95, 4, 0.36, "Time after practice →", { font: "light", size: 15, color: "muted" });
        s.text(X(1) - 4, 5.95, 4, 0.36, "Illustrative shape, not data", { font: "light", size: 13, color: "muted", align: "right" });
        s.source("Brief dip: Sudo et al. 2022 · Lingering: Wittbrodt & Millard-Stafford 2018; Fullagar et al. 2015", { y: 6.3, w: 11.8, refs: ["sudo2022", "wittbrodt2018", "fullagar2015"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "application", time: 65,
      notes: `So what do we do with this? We don't need to ban teaching after practice. We need to change the order.

Straight off the field, the first job is water, food and somewhere to sit. Keep anything said in that window to logistics: when, where, what to bring.

Once they've had a drink and cooled down, that's the time to teach the thing that takes real thinking, a film clip, a rehab progression, a new play.

For Athletic Medicine and nutrition this is already second nature. The ask is for the rest of us to build the same order into meetings and film.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Give the first minutes to water, not the whiteboard.", { y: 1.2, w: 11.5, h: 0.9, size: 44 });
        const rows = [
          ["Off the field", "Water, food, a seat. Logistics only."],
          ["Once they’ve cooled down", "Teach the thing that takes thinking."],
        ];
        rows.forEach(([when, todo], i) => {
          const y = 2.85 + i * 1.6;
          s.line(0.75, y, 12.58, y, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
          s.text(0.75, y + 0.38, 4.0, 0.9, when, { font: "cond", size: 24, color: "muted", lineH: 1.1 });
          s.text(4.9, y + 0.3, 7.68, 0.9, todo, { font: "light", size: 32, lineH: 1.1 });
        });
        s.line(0.75, 6.05, 12.58, 6.05, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        s.cite("wittbrodt2018");
      },
    },
    {
      role: "takeaway", time: 45,
      notes: `The line to take away: refuel first, then teach.

The fog after practice is shorter than we thought. What keeps athletes slow is the water they haven't had and the sleep they didn't get, and both are things we can plan around.

Question for the room: what do we try to teach in the first five minutes after practice? Think about film, rehab instructions, academic check-ins and announcements. Which of those could wait ten minutes, and which could move earlier in the day?`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Refuel first. Then teach.", { y: 1.3, w: 9.5, h: 1.3, size: 80 });
        s.text(5.6, 4.15, 6.98, 1.8, "What do we try to teach in the first five minutes after practice?", { font: "medium", size: 32, color: "accent", lineH: 1.15, align: "right" });
      },
    },
  ],
};

