// 03 · Notification Fatigue: every extra message makes the next one weaker; batch them.
// Composition: hook = one plain statement across the slide; takeaway = the question is the
// whole slide; alignment = left.

module.exports = {
  num: 3,
  slug: "notification-fatigue",
  title: "Notification Fatigue",
  category: "Contextual Engineering & Education Delivery",
  palette: "sand",
  hero: { layout: "diminishing-stack", concept: "A stack of six message bars hugging the right margin, each fainter than the last: attention per message falls as the count rises" },
  compose: { hook: "statement", takeaway: "question-only", align: "left" },
  oneIdea: "Attention is shared across every message an athlete gets. Send fewer, at set times, and each one counts for more.",
  question: "Who owns the total number of messages our athletes get in a day?",
  caveats: `None of this research was done on athletes. The 30% figure comes from primary care clinicians responding to electronic health record reminders (Ancker et al. 2017), and it is per additional reminder within one patient visit; present it as a pattern from another high-volume setting, not a measurement of our athletes. The notification study (Stothart et al. 2015) is a single lab experiment on an attention task. The batching trial (Fitz et al. 2019) lasted two weeks, ran in India with adult smartphone users, and relied on self-report; batching hourly did little, and switching notifications off entirely raised anxiety and fear of missing out, so "send nothing" is not the lesson. Urgent safety and schedule changes (weather, lightning, a moved bus) should still go out right away. The stack on slide 3 is a drawn shape, labeled as illustrative.`,
  sources: [
    { id: "stothart2015", cite: "Stothart C, Mitchum A, Yehnert C. The attentional cost of receiving a cell phone notification. J Exp Psychol Hum Percept Perform. 2015;41(4):893-897. doi:10.1037/xhp0000100", grade: "single lab experiment", found: "external", supports: "Notifications alone disrupted performance on an attention-demanding task, even when participants did not interact with the phone." },
    { id: "ancker2017", cite: "Ancker JS, Edwards A, Nosal S, Hauser D, Mauer E, Kaushal R; with the HITEC Investigators. Effects of workload, work complexity, and repeated alerts on alert fatigue in a clinical decision support system. BMC Med Inform Decis Mak. 2017;17(1):36. doi:10.1186/s12911-017-0430-8", grade: "retrospective cohort (112 primary care clinicians)", found: "external", supports: "'Likelihood of reminder acceptance dropped by 30% for each additional reminder received per encounter'; more repeated reminders also lowered acceptance." },
    { id: "fitz2019", cite: "Fitz N, Kushlev K, Jagannathan R, Lewis T, Paliwal D, Ariely D. Batching smartphone notifications can improve well-being. Comput Human Behav. 2019;101:84-94. doi:10.1016/j.chb.2019.07.016", grade: "randomized field experiment (n = 237, two weeks)", found: "external", supports: "Notifications batched three times a day lowered stress and inattention and raised perceived productivity; hourly batching did little; no notifications raised anxiety and fear of missing out." },
  ],
  slides: [
    {
      role: "hook", time: 45,
      notes: `Think about one athlete's phone on a Tuesday. A lift time change from Athletic Performance. A hydration reminder from nutrition. A tutoring reminder from academics. A rehab time from Athletic Medicine. The position group chat. Compliance. Each of those messages is reasonable, and each unit sent it because it matters.

The athlete doesn't get our message. They get all of them, at once, on top of everything else on their phone. Today is about what that volume does to each message.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Every unit sends the important message, and the athlete gets all of them.", { y: 1.75, w: 11.6, h: 3.1, size: 62 });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "problem", time: 60,
      notes: `Two findings, from outside sport, that are worth knowing.

First, a lab study found that just getting a notification, without picking up the phone, hurt performance on an attention task. The cost starts before anyone reads anything.

Second, a study of more than a hundred primary care clinicians looked at how often they acted on electronic reminders. With each additional reminder in a single patient visit, the likelihood they accepted a reminder dropped by about thirty percent. Repeats made it worse.

These are clinicians, not athletes. But the pattern is the one we'd expect: more messages, and each one gets less.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Every message costs attention, opened or not.", { y: 1.2, w: 8, h: 1.75, size: 54 });
        const cols = [
          ["A phone that buzzes", "A notification alone hurt focus on an attention task, even unanswered."],
          ["One more reminder", "Each extra reminder in a visit made clinicians 30% less likely to accept one."],
        ];
        cols.forEach(([lab, t], i) => {
          const x = 0.75 + i * 6.0;
          s.label(lab, { x, y: 3.55, w: 5.3 });
          s.text(x, 3.95, 5.3, 1.9, t, { font: "light", size: 24, lineH: 1.2 });
        });
        s.source("Stothart et al. 2015, lab task · Ancker et al. 2017, primary care clinicians", { y: 6.0, w: 11.8, refs: ["stothart2015", "ancker2017"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "insight", time: 70,
      notes: `So here's the idea. Attention is shared across every message an athlete gets, not added up. Each bar is a message, and each one gets a little less than the one before. This is a drawing of the pattern, not a plot of data.

That changes how we should think about our own messages. When nutrition sends a reminder, it competes with Athletic Performance's reminder, and with mine. None of us sees the total. The athlete does.

So the fourth reminder about hydration isn't free. It costs a little attention from every message that comes after it, including the one that really matters.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("The more we send, the less each one gets.", { y: 1.4, w: 6.6, h: 2.6, size: 56 });
        s.lead("Attention is shared across every message, not added up.", { y: 4.45, w: 6.0, h: 1.0, size: 22 });
        const x = 8.15, w = 4.43, h = 0.44, gap = 0.2, y0 = 1.55;
        const ops = [1, 0.72, 0.5, 0.34, 0.22, 0.13];
        ops.forEach((op, i) => {
          s.rect(x, y0 + i * (h + gap), w, h, { fill: "accent", opacity: op });
        });
        s.text(x - 1.6, y0 + 0.02, 1.45, 0.4, "1st", { font: "cond", size: 18, color: "ink", align: "right" });
        s.text(x - 1.6, y0 + 5 * (h + gap) + 0.02, 1.45, 0.4, "6th", { font: "cond", size: 18, color: "muted", align: "right" });
        s.text(x, y0 + 6 * (h + gap) + 0.02, w, 0.36, "Illustrative shape, not data", { font: "light", size: 13, color: "muted", align: "right" });
        s.source("Pattern: Ancker et al. 2017", { y: 6.0, w: 6, refs: ["ancker2017"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "application", time: 65,
      notes: `What can we do on Monday? Agree, across units, on a few set times a day when messages go to athletes.

There's a randomized trial on exactly this. People whose phone notifications arrived in three batches a day felt less stressed and more attentive, and said they got more done. Batching every hour barely helped. And turning notifications off completely backfired: people got more anxious about what they were missing.

So the goal isn't silence. It's predictable. Urgent things, like weather or a moved bus, still go out right away. Everything else waits for the next send time.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline(["Agree across units on ", { t: "three set times a day", color: "accent" }, " for messages to athletes."], { y: 1.9, w: 10.6, h: 1.8, size: 44 });
        s.line(0.75, 4.15, 12.58, 4.15, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        s.lead("Batching three times a day lowered stress. Hourly batches barely helped, and switching alerts off raised anxiety.", { y: 4.45, w: 9.6, h: 1.3, size: 24 });
        s.source("Fitz et al. 2019, randomized field trial", { y: 6.0, w: 8, refs: ["fitz2019"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "takeaway", time: 45,
      notes: `The line to take away: send fewer, at set times, and each one counts for more.

Right now, every unit owns its own messages and nobody owns the total. That's the gap.

So here's the question for the room: who owns the total number of messages our athletes get in a day? It might be a person, a shared calendar, or just an agreement between units. Let people answer honestly; the first answer is usually "nobody".`,
      build(s) {
        s.text(0.75, 1.9, 11.0, 3.2, "Who owns the total number of messages our athletes get in a day?", { font: "medium", size: 54, color: "accent", lineH: 1.12 });
      },
    },
  ],
};

