// 04 · The Flipped Classroom Model: move the telling out of the room, keep the room for the
// hard part. Composition: hook = two plain columns, what we assume vs what studies find;
// takeaway = line centered, question beneath; alignment = center (insight and takeaway).

module.exports = {
  num: 4,
  slug: "the-flipped-classroom-model",
  title: "The Flipped Classroom Model",
  category: "Contextual Engineering & Education Delivery",
  palette: "slate",
  hero: { layout: "swapped-timelines", concept: "Two timelines across before / in the meeting / after: the same two pieces of work, shifted so that using the content, not hearing it, happens in the room" },
  compose: { hook: "assume-vs-measured", takeaway: "centered", align: "center" },
  oneIdea: "Send the telling ahead, check it, and keep the full meeting for using it. Flipping only helps when it's set up that way.",
  question: "Which of our meetings could start where the pre-read ends?",
  caveats: `The flipped-classroom evidence comes from secondary and college courses, not staff meetings or athlete education sessions; treat the transfer as reasonable, not proven. The gains are small: Hedges' g of 0.35 across 272 comparisons, 0.24 when restricted to adequately powered studies, and both meta-analyses flag publication bias and large differences between studies (Låg & Sæle 2019; van Alten et al. 2019). Student satisfaction barely moved. The two design features with support, keeping the full face-to-face time and checking preparation with a quiz, come from moderator analyses, which are correlational. If someone says "so we can cut meeting time": van Alten et al. found the opposite; flipped classes that cut class time did worse than those that kept it. The timelines on slide 3 are a diagram of the model, not data.`,
  sources: [
    { id: "lag2019", cite: "Låg T, Sæle RG. Does the flipped classroom improve student learning and satisfaction? A systematic review and meta-analysis. AERA Open. 2019;5(3). doi:10.1177/2332858419870489", grade: "systematic review and meta-analysis", found: "external", supports: "Small learning effect (g = 0.35, k = 272); 0.24 in adequately powered studies (k = 90); satisfaction g = 0.16, likely inflated by publication bias; some support that testing student preparation adds slightly to learning." },
    { id: "vanalten2019", cite: "van Alten DCD, Phielix C, Janssen J, Kester L. Effects of flipping the classroom on learning outcomes and satisfaction: a meta-analysis. Educ Res Rev. 2019;28:100281. doi:10.1016/j.edurev.2019.05.003", grade: "meta-analysis (114 studies)", found: "external", supports: "Small positive effect on learning, none on satisfaction; larger effects when face-to-face time was not reduced or when quizzes were added." },
  ],
  slides: [
    {
      role: "hook", time: 45,
      notes: `You've probably heard the pitch for the flipped classroom. Send the content ahead, a video or a reading, and use the meeting for discussion instead of a lecture. A lot of us have tried some version of it: "read this before Tuesday."

Here's the honest version before we start. The idea is sound, and it's been studied a lot in schools and colleges. But the gain is smaller than the pitch, and it only shows up when the setup is right. That setup is what's worth five minutes today.`,
      build(s) {
        s.label("What we assume", { x: 0.75, y: 2.05, w: 5.3 });
        s.text(0.75, 2.5, 5.4, 2.8, "Send it ahead and the meeting gets better.", { font: "light", size: 40, lineH: 1.12 });
        s.line(6.67, 2.1, 6.67, 5.6, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        s.label("What studies find", { x: 7.2, y: 2.05, w: 5.3 });
        s.text(7.2, 2.5, 5.38, 2.8, "A small gain, and only with the right setup.", { font: "light", size: 40, color: "accent", lineH: 1.12 });
        s.source("Låg & Sæle 2019 · van Alten et al. 2019", { x: 7.2, y: 5.85, w: 5.38, refs: ["lag2019", "vanalten2019"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "problem", time: 60,
      notes: `Two big reviews pooled the classroom studies. One gathered 272 comparisons of flipped and lecture-based classes. Flipped classes came out ahead, but by a small margin, an effect size of about a third of a standard deviation. When they kept only the studies large enough to trust, the edge shrank to about a quarter. Students weren't much happier with flipped classes either.

So flipping isn't magic. Lots of flipped classes did no better than lectures, and some did worse.

The useful question is: what did the ones that worked have in common? Both reviews point the same way.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Flipping helps a little, when it’s set up right.", { y: 1.2, w: 11.5, h: 0.9, size: 44 });
        s.figure("0.35", { x: 0.75, y: 2.55, w: 4.2, h: 1.75, size: 120, color: "muted", refs: ["lag2019"] });
        s.label("All 272 comparisons", { x: 0.8, y: 4.4, w: 4.2 });
        s.figure("0.24", { x: 5.3, y: 2.55, w: 4.2, h: 1.75, size: 120, color: "ink", refs: ["lag2019"] });
        s.label("Only studies big enough to trust", { x: 5.35, y: 4.4, w: 4.6 });
        s.text(0.75, 5.0, 11.0, 0.6, "Both are small effects. Satisfaction barely moved.", { font: "light", size: 26, lineH: 1.1 });
        s.source("Effect sizes (Hedges’ g) for learning: Låg & Sæle 2019 · Satisfaction: van Alten et al. 2019", { y: 6.0, w: 11.8, refs: ["lag2019", "vanalten2019"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "insight", time: 75,
      notes: `Here's what flipping actually changes. It doesn't add new work. It moves the same two pieces.

In a traditional meeting, top row, we spend the room hearing the content, and the hard part, using it, happens later, alone, if it happens at all.

Flipped, bottom row, the hearing happens before, on their own time. The meeting is where they use it: work the problem, ask the question, make the decision. That's the part that benefits from having the coach, the trainer, or the dietitian in the room.

So the point of a flipped meeting isn't less time together. It's better use of it.`,
      build(s) {
